WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 226: Sweden: Gustavus Adolphus, the Lion of the North, and the Battle of Breitenfeld

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:21.840
[Rebecca] Today, our European Odyssey travels to the frozen pine forests and rocky shores of the Baltic, where a brilliant young monarch transformed a sparsely populated kingdom of just one million people into one of the most terrifying military superpowers in history.

3
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.200
[Rebecca] King Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden.

4
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:44.800
[George] His realm was poor, backward, and sparsely populated, with a total population barely exceeding one million peasants and fishermen.

5
00:00:44.800 --> 00:01:03.760
[George] Worse, Sweden was locked in simultaneous, desperate wars against three formidable rivals: Christian IV of Denmark, who occupied southern Swedish lands, Tsar Michael Romanov of Russia, and King Sigismund III of Poland, Gustavus's own cousin, who claimed the Swedish crown.

6
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:10.400
[George] Rather than surrendering, young Gustavus demonstrated a maturity and intellect far beyond his years.

7
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:24.720
[George] Fluent in eight languages, deeply read in classical history, and blessed with towering physical strength and charismatic courage, he allied himself with the brilliant statesman Axel Oxenstierna, who became his lifelong chancellor.

8
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:39.680
[George] Together, they ended the Danish war with painful concessions, defeated the Russians to win total control of the Gulf of Finland, and battled the Polish Commonwealth across Livonia, turning Sweden into a unified, fortified regional power.

9
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:54.880
[George] Axel Oxenstierna, who was eleven years older than the king, possessed a cool, methodical temperament that perfectly complemented Gustavus's passionate, impulsive energy, creating one of the most effective governing partnerships in European history.

10
00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:05.320
[George] In 1617, Gustavus concluded the Treaty of Stolbovo with Russia, cutting Moscow completely off from Baltic maritime trade and boasting to the Swedish Riksdag.

11
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:10.200
[George] Now the Russian cannot launch a single boat on the Baltic without our permission.

12
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:17.480
[George] Gustavus Adolphus recognized that Sweden could never match the massive armies of Spain or France through sheer numbers.

13
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:23.240
[George] It had to outthink, outmaneuver, and outshoot them through technological and tactical innovation.

14
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:37.240
[George] In an era when armies relied on clumsy, mercenary pike squares forty ranks deep that moved like ponderous behemoths, Gustavus revolutionized military doctrine, earning the title, the father of modern warfare.

15
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:46.600
[George] He formed national conscripted regiments motivated by patriotic pride and Protestant religious devotion, rather than unreliable foreign mercenaries.

16
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:57.160
[George] He reduced the depth of infantry formations to just six ranks, arming his soldiers with lightweight wheel-lock muskets that could be loaded and fired in synchronized rolling volleys.

17
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.480
[George] Most revolutionary was his invention of light, mobile artillery.

18
00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:17.320
[George] Instead of heavy siege guns that bogged down in mud, Gustavus cast standardized three-pounder bronze cannons that could be drawn by a single horse and operated directly alongside the infantry, firing devastating canisters shot into enemy formations.

19
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:23.080
[George] For his cavalry, he banned the cowardly practice of riding up to shoot pistols and retreating.

20
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:31.080
[George] Swedish horsemen were commanded to charge at full gallop with drawn sabres, riding straight into the enemy with terrifying shock power.

21
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:44.920
[George] Gustavus's infantrymen were drilled to fire in coordinated ranks, the first rank kneeling, the second crouching, and the third standing, unleashing a simultaneous wall of lead known as the salvo that shattered enemy morale.

22
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:58.560
[George] The Swedish artillery train was standardized by Gustavus so that any cannon crew could operate any gun, with pre-packaged powder charges in paper cartridges that accelerated the rate of fire to unprecedented speeds.

23
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:06.400
[George] By 1630, Central Europe was consumed by the Thirty Years' War, the bloodiest religious conflict in human history.

24
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:25.200
[George] The Holy Roman Emperor Ferdinand II and the Catholic League, led by the undefeated general Count Tilly and Albrecht von Wallenstein, had utterly crushed Protestant resistance across Bohemia, Austria, and Northern Germany, executing Protestant princes and burning hundreds of towns to ashes.

25
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.680
[George] Protestantism was on the brink of total extinction on the European continent.

26
00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:35.200
[George] On July 6, 1630, Gustavus Adolphus made his fateful move.

27
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:42.160
[George] He landed an army of 15,000 Swedish veterans on the windswept Baltic island of Eusdem in northern Germany.

28
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:50.880
[George] As he stepped ashore onto German soil, the king dropped to his knees on the sand and offered a fervent prayer to God for the deliverance of the oppressed.

29
00:04:50.880 --> 00:05:00.800
[George] German pamphlets hailed him as the Lion of the North, fulfilling ancient prophecies of a golden deliverer from the Arctic snows who would break the chains of Catholic oppression.

30
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.760
[George] When Gustavus landed in Germany, his army maintained strict discipline.

31
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:15.920
[George] Soldiers were required to attend morning and evening prayers, plundering of civilian farms was punishable by death, and blasphemy and swearing were strictly forbidden.

32
00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:28.960
[George] Gustavus's landing at Penimund was financed in part by a secret treaty with Catholic France, negotiated by Cardinal Richelieu, who cared far more about weakening the Habsburgs than about religious allegiances.

33
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:43.240
[George] On September 17, 1631, outside the village of Breitenfeld near Leipzig, Gustavus Adolphus faced Count Tilly, the veteran Catholic commander who had never lost a pitched battle in his entire career.

34
00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:52.360
[George] Tilly deployed 42,000 men in massive, traditional Spanish tercios, solid squares of armored pikemen flanked by musketeers.

35
00:05:52.360 --> 00:06:02.680
[George] When the battle began, the Catholic cavalry smashed the Protestant Saxon allies on the Swedish left flank, sending thousands of Saxons fleeing in panic from the field.

36
00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:08.840
[George] Tilly believed victory was his and ordered his massive tercios forward to crush the remaining Swedes.

37
00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.520
[George] But Gustavus displayed breathtaking tactical brilliance.

38
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:23.960
[George] Using his disciplined, flexible formations, he refused his left flank, pivoted his entire line under fire, and brought his mobile three-pounder artillery into point blank range.

39
00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:33.640
[George] The Swedish musketeers unleashed devastating rolling volleys into the packed Catholic squares, while the Swedish artillery ripped bloody avenues through the Tercios.

40
00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:42.200
[George] Gustavus himself led a thunderous cavalry charge into Tilly's rear, capturing the Catholic artillery and turning their own guns upon them.

41
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:50.120
[George] Tilly's army was annihilated, Breitenfeld shocked Europe, establishing Sweden as the premier military force on the continent.

42
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:56.280
[George] At Breitenfeld, Count Tilly's veteran soldiers were astonished by the speed of the Swedish maneuvers.

43
00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:03.880
[George] The Swedish soldiers could reload and fire three volleys for every single shot discharged by the cumbersome Catholic musketeers.

44
00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:16.880
[George] Breitenfeld was the first major battle in which dynamic linear firepower decisively overcame the static mass of dense pike columns, marking the definitive birth of modern battlefield tactics in Western military history.

45
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:24.560
[George] The miraculous triumph at Breitenfeld transformed Gustavus Adolphus into the undisputed master of Central Europe.

46
00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:35.760
[George] In a whirlwind winter campaign that astonished contemporary military observers, the Swedish army swept across the Rhineland and Franconia, capturing fortress after fortress.

47
00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:45.520
[George] German Protestant cities threw open their gates, welcoming Gustavus with ringing church bells, triumphal arches, and choirs singing hymns of deliverance.

48
00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:55.200
[George] In the spring of 1632, Gustavus forced the crossing of the Lech River under heavy fire, where Count Tilly was mortally wounded by a cannonball.

49
00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:09.040
[George] The Swedish king marched triumphantly into Munich, the capital of his archnemesis the Elector of Bavaria, walking unarmed through the city streets and strictly forbidding his soldiers from looting or harming the Catholic population.

50
00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.480
[George] Catholic Europe was gripped by existential panic.

51
00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:19.840
[George] There seemed to be no fortress, no general, and no army capable of stopping the unstoppable Swedish juggernaut.

52
00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:33.920
[George] During his occupation of Bavaria, Gustavus visited the workshops of German watchmakers and instrument makers in Augsburg and Nuremberg, commissioning exquisite mechanical curiosities and scientific instruments to send back to Stockholm.

53
00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:47.040
[George] The Swedish king's reputation for personal magnanimity and honor was celebrated across Protestant Europe, earning him the affectionate nickname, the Golden King from German peasants whom he protected from mercenary marauders.

54
00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:56.920
[George] In desperation, Holy Roman Emperor Ferdinand I I recalled the ruthless military genius Albrecht von Wallenstein to raise a massive new imperial army.

55
00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:07.080
[George] On the foggy morning of November 16, 1632, the two greatest military minds of the age clashed at the Battle of Lutzen, near Leipzig.

56
00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:21.160
[George] Gustavus rode before his troops on his white horse, wearing a simple buff leather coat rather than iron armor due to an old musket wound in his neck, leading them in singing Martin Luther's battle hymn, A Mighty Fortress is Our God.

57
00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:28.840
[George] As the battle raged, a dense, blinding fog settled over the battlefield, mixed with choking black gunpowder smoke.

58
00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:35.800
[George] Leading a cavalry charge to relieve his beleaguered infantry, Gustavus became separated from his men in the fog.

59
00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:40.280
[George] A stray imperial bullet struck his left arm, shattering the bone.

60
00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:45.960
[George] As his horse bolted toward the imperial lines, another musket ball struck him in the back.

61
00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:50.840
[George] Imperial cuirassiers surrounded the fallen monarch, demanding his identity.

62
00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:59.000
[George] Gustavus gasping his final words, I am the king of Sweden, and I seal my religion and the liberty of Germany with my blood.

63
00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.960
[George] He was shot through the head and stripped of his clothes.

64
00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:15.320
[George] At Lutzen, Wallenstein had ordered his men to dig deep drainage ditches along the road to serve as trenches, turning the muddy field into a deadly killing zone that exacted a gruesome toll on the advancing Swedish regiments.

65
00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:28.040
[George] After the Battle of Lutzen, Gustavus's body was recovered from the mud by his grief-stricken officers, embalmed in the town church of Weissenfels, and transported in a solemn funeral procession back to Sweden.

66
00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:35.640
[George] When news of Gustavus Adolphus's death reached his soldiers, grief turned into uncontrollable, vengeful fury.

67
00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:48.240
[George] Led by Duke Bernhard of Saxe Weimar, the Swedish regiments surged forward in a ferocious, irresistible assault, driving Wallenstein's army from the field and securing a bloody victory at Lutzen.

68
00:10:44.520 --> 00:11:00.400
[George] Under the brilliant leadership of Chancellor Axel Oxenstierna, Sweden remained at the center of European affairs, fighting on for another 16 years until the Peace of Westphalia in 1648.

69
00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:11.920
[George] The treaty cemented Swedish possession of Western Pomerania, Bremen, and Stettin, confirming Sweden's status as a great power, the Stormikstid, or Age of Greatness.

70
00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:26.000
[George] Gustavus Adolphus died at the young age of 37, but his military innovations permanently transformed the art of warfare, while his defense of religious freedom guaranteed the permanent survival of Protestantism in northern Europe.

71
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:35.760
[George] To this day, he remains the only Swedish monarch officially bestowed with the title, the Great by the Swedish Parliament, immortalized as the Lion of the North.

72
00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:47.600
[George] The Peace of Westphalia established Sweden as an imperial guarantor of the Holy Roman Empire with seats in the imperial diet, cementing the Baltic Sea as a Swedish dominion for nearly a century.

73
00:11:47.600 --> 00:12:01.280
[George] Gustavus Adolphus was laid to rest in the Ridarholm Church in Stockholm, where his green marble sarcophagus remains a national shrine, honoring the warrior king who lifted Sweden to the pinnacle of European glory.

74
00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.800
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

